 ## Overview
-Binary and Soft Classification is a family of volume rendering methods that map scalar voxel intensities to optical properties to determine visibility. It solves the problem of selecting which data contributes to the final image by defining inclusion criteria, ranging from discrete hard thresholds to continuous smooth transitions. It sets itself apart from advanced siblings like [Probabilistic and Statistical Modeling](5932-probabilistic-and-statistical-modeling.md) by relying on deterministic, user-defined mappings rather than statistical distributions, and from [Data Feature-Based Classification](5934-data-feature-based-classification.md) by typically operating on raw scalar values rather than complex derived feature spaces [G].
